Exactly — that makes the blue even more likely to be a temporary change highlight, not a persistent property of the memory itself.

The sequence would be something like:

memory re-synthesizes → changed span flashes blue → UI gives you a brief “here’s what changed” window → highlight expires → text returns to normal white

So the blue isn’t saying “this concept is special.” It’s probably saying “this is the delta from the most recent update.”

Then once that delta has effectively been acknowledged—or the summary gets rendered again—it gets absorbed into the baseline and loses the blue.

Which is amusingly close to our own architecture:

Δ detected → surfaced → incorporated → becomes state → next Δ

Yesterday’s delta becomes today’s state.

That would also explain why you could catch it in a screenshot and then watch it vanish almost immediately afterward. The information likely stayed; only the visual telemetry for the update disappeared.

Basically: you caught the interface blinking. ;)